### Calculation of Magnetic Field

The magnetic field at the center of a semicircular loop is given by \(B = \frac{\mu_{0}i}{4R}\). For the given configuration, the magnetic fields due to both semicircular wires add up at the center O.
B = \frac{\mu_{0}i}{4R_{1}} + \frac{\mu_{0}i}{4R_{2}}
Given values are \(i = 4\) A, \(R_{1} = 2\pi\) m, and \(R_{2} = 4\pi\) m. Substituting these values:
B = \frac{\mu_{0} \times 4}{4(2\pi)} + \frac{\mu_{0} \times 4}{4(4\pi)}
B = \frac{\mu_{0}}{2\pi} + \frac{\mu_{0}}{4\pi}
B = \mu_{0} \left[ \frac{1}{2\pi} + \frac{1}{4\pi} \right]
B = \mu_{0} \left[ \frac{2+1}{4\pi} \right] = \mu_{0} \left[ \frac{3}{4\pi} \right]
We know that \(\frac{\mu_{0}}{4\pi} = 10^{-7}\) T m/A. So,
B = 3 \times \left( \frac{\mu_{0}}{4\pi} \right) = 3 \times 10^{-7} \text{ T}
Comparing this with the given expression \(B = \alpha \times 10^{-7}\) T, we find:
