(1-x)^{2008}[1+x+x^2]^{2007}
=(1-x)(1-x)^{2007}[1+x+x^2]^{2007}
=(1-x)[(1-x)(1+x+x^2)]^{2007}
=(1-x)[1-x^3]^{2007}
The general term in the expansion of \((1-x^3)^{2007}\) is \(T_{r+1} = {^{2007}\mathrm{C}_r} (1)^{2007-r} (-x^3)^r = {^{2007}\mathrm{C}_r} (-1)^r x^{3r}\).
So, the given expression becomes:
(1-x) \sum_{r=0}^{2007} {^{2007}\mathrm{C}_r} (-1)^r x^{3r}
= \sum_{r=0}^{2007} {^{2007}\mathrm{C}_r} (-1)^r x^{3r} - \sum_{r=0}^{2007} {^{2007}\mathrm{C}_r} (-1)^r x^{3r+1}
For the coefficient of \(x^{2012}\):
From the first sum, we set the power of \(x\) to 2012:
3r = 2012 \implies r = \frac{2012}{3}
From the second sum, we set the power of \(x\) to 2012:
3r + 1 = 2012 \implies 3r = 2011 \implies r = \frac{2011}{3}
In both cases, \(r\) is not an integer. Since \(r\) must be an integer for the terms to exist in the binomial expansion, no such term with \(x^{2012}\) exists.
\\therefore Coefficient is zero

